<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phuket</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phuket is the largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, in the Andaman Sea off the west coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. Long known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mining, which drew many Chinese settlers, it was attacked by Burmese forces in 1785, when its chief town, Thalang, was successfully defended under the leadership of two women, Thao Thep Kasattri and Thao Si Sunthon. Today it is a province and a major tourist destination.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phuket.jpg" alt="Phuket" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bennypc · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phuket_Aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Phuket lies in the Andaman Sea off the west coast of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and is joined to Phang Nga province in the north by the Sarasin Bridge. The province consists of Phuket Island and 32 smaller islands, and covers 547 square kilometres. A mountain range runs along the western side of the island, part of a larger range that stretches about 440 kilometres from the Kra Isthmus. The name comes from the Malay word bukit, meaning hill, which is how the island looks from a distance. It was formerly called Thalang, from the Malay telong, meaning cape, and Western charts knew it as Junk Ceylon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Phuket was historically inhabited by Austronesian Malays before the Thai settled it through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nam-tien">southward expansion</a> of Siamese kingdoms. It lay on a major trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and ships from Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> mention it in their logs, though no European nation colonised it. The Portuguese Fernão Mendes Pinto, who arrived in Siam in 1545, calls it Junk Ceylon and describes it as a port of call for supplies, though he notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and difficult seas were reducing its importance by the mid-16th century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a> mining was central to the economy from the 16th century until its decline in the 20th, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> also became important. In 1785 Burmese forces invaded, and the Two Heroines Monument in Thalang District commemorates Thao Thep Kasattri and Thao Sri Sunthon, who rallied the people against them. The Thalang National Museum was established in 1985 for the 200th anniversary of the Thalang War.</p><p>Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a>, who reigned from 1868 to 1910, Phuket became the administrative centre for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-producing southern provinces of Siam. In 1876 Chinese laborers in the tin mines of Phuket and neighbouring provinces rose in unrest, which was linked to falling tin prices and the government's fiscal policies. Monthon Phuket was dissolved in 1933, making Phuket a separate province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Phuket is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> divided into three districts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> is the predominant religion, and about a fifth of the population follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, mainly descendants of the island's original Austronesian inhabitants. Many others are of Chinese ancestry, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> miners who came in the 19th century, among them the Peranakan community known locally as Phuket Babas. Tourism developed from the 1980s around the beaches of Patong, Karon and Kata, and a 45-metre Big Buddha overlooks Chalong Bay. The tsunami of 26 December 2004 devastated the island's west coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phuket province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…broad front in what Thai histories call the Nine Armies War; the Siamese defeated the main thrust at Lat Ya near Kanchanaburi, the attack on Thalang on <b>Phuket</b> island was repulsed, and later Burmese campaigns, the last major one against Thalang in 1809–1810, failed to reverse the result.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
